# 14x11 top-down desk for a NORTH-facing seat -- one whose occupant sits on
# the NEAR side with their back to the viewer (`layout.desk_facings`, read by
# `desk_facing_at`). Same 14 px width and same BOTTOM THREE rows as
# `desk.sprite` (front edge + legs); the two extra rows are all above, which
# is the entire point. (Three is the BLIT offset below, not a row count.)
#
# WHY a second sprite rather than a taller shared one: a back-turned occupant
# is anchored at `desk.y` and y-sorts IN FRONT of their desk, so on the base
# sprite their head lands exactly on rows 2-3 -- the screen -- and covers it.
# Here the monitor stands tall enough that its UPPER screen row sits at
# `desk.y-1`, clear of the head, so the screen and its tool-coloured glow are
# visible on precisely the desks whose occupant is looking at one. A
# far-seated desk keeps `desk.sprite`: its occupant is BEHIND the monitor, so a
# raised screen would show a display nobody in the scene can see.
#
# TWO extra rows, so `blit_y = desk.y - 3`. One MORE row was the first version:
# it put BOTH screen rows above `desk.y` and therefore both clear of the head,
# which reads better on an occupied desk but leaves the monitor visibly floating
# a row off the wood on an empty one. Owner picked the lower monitor
# (facing north 的电脑可以整体往下移动1px吗), so the lower screen row now lands
# INSIDE the surface band at `desk.y+0` and an occupant's hair covers it —
# a deliberate trade of screen area for a monitor that sits on its desk.
#
# That lower row therefore carries flanking WOOD, exactly as every screen row of
# `desk.sprite` does: a monitor row inside the surface band has desk either side
# of it. Only the rows ABOVE the band are transparent-flanked. Get this wrong in
# either direction and the two desks stop being the same size — which shipped
# once (facing south 4px vs facing north 6px, then 7px).
#
# The desk SURFACE runs up behind the screen rows rather than stopping under
# them, so the monitor is drawn standing IN the desk rather than perched at its
# back edge -- wood flanks it at rows 2-4 and only the top two rows rise clear,
# the same 1-2 rows of proud monitor the base desk already reads with. Two
# earlier passes got this wrong: 14x12 with a 1px stand neck floating the
# monitor a whole row above the wood (owner-rejected as too tall, and the neck
# was hidden behind the head anyway), then 14x11 with the surface starting
# below the chin, which left the monitor touching the wood but never
# overlapping it. The desk reads 2 rows deeper than `desk.sprite` as a result;
# that is a VISUAL overhang north, which the shallow south-strip footprint
# (`ground_y: End`, invariant #6) does not care about.
#
# The monitor block's ROW ROLES are load-bearing, not tidiness: from this
# sprite's own top row, 0-1 are casing, 2-3 glass, 4 the chin, and
# `effects::paint_screen_glow` lights all three (`paint_screen_idle` the glass
# rows only)
# (`SCREEN_CASING_ROWS`/`SCREEN_GLASS_ROWS`/`SCREEN_CHIN_ROW`, cols 3..=10 and
# 4..=9). Reshuffling them, or dropping a casing row to save height, lights the
# wrong pixels. `desk.sprite` satisfies the same roles from ITS top row; the two
# blocks are no longer pixel-identical, so do not restore that as the check.
@frame 0
